    "narrative": "The offensive core for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on three high-ceiling plays today: **James Wood** (78.42), **Austin Riley** (74.11), and **Tyler Soderstrom** (73.70). **James Wood** is our premier play, benefiting from an elite xwOBA of 0.426 and a significant stolen base opportunity against Eduardo Rodriguez, who struggles with a slow delivery to the plate. Similarly, **Austin Riley** exploits a favorable park environment and a soft matchup against Trevor Rogers, while **Tyler Soderstrom** is primed for a breakout performance batting second against Zebby Matthews, where the combination of high-value lineup placement and a strong platoon split maximizes his daily efficiency score. **Brice Turang** also warrants attention as a top-tier start (71.09); his ability to leverage a high-volume SB environment against the vulnerable arm of Sugano makes him an essential component of our speed-weighted strategy.\n\nOur most difficult decision of the morning involved keeping **Seiya Suzuki** (45.87) on the bench. While his historical output remains relevant, the optimizer penalized him heavily due to a daunting matchup against Jared Jones and a unfavorable park factor that cratered his base projection. He narrowly missed the cut over **Jose Caballero**, who\u2014despite a lower base score\u2014retains more utility in our speed-centric model due to his superior stolen base potential and a favorable dynamic platoon advantage against the lefty Jes\u00fas Luzardo. We are also closely monitoring **Gabriel Moreno** and **Cole Young**, as both remain heavily reliant on the \"Lineup Pending\" assumption; should their actual batting orders shift downward, their efficiency scores will likely regress toward the marginal threshold. With no major weather concerns outside of a minor risk for **Edwin Arroyo**, the primary risk remains the high-strikeout potential of the opposing pitchers facing our lower-tier starters like **Henry Bolte** and **Josh Naylor**.",
